Yes. There are two different insights tangled together there, and separating them makes the whole thing much cleaner.

First, the counterfactual error: when someone imagines “me, but born the other sex,” they often preserve everything they like about their present self and then bolt on an idealized version of the other sex. That is not really the counterfactual.

A more realistic model is:

current dispositions
+ different biology
+ different childhood treatment
+ different peer incentives
+ different expectations
+ different experiences
= alternate developmental trajectory

So she probably would not be literally the exact same person as a man, but she also would not magically become her fantasy archetype. Likewise, a man imagining himself as a woman tends to underestimate how decades of different treatment and embodiment would change the trajectory. “Look at your relatives and the distribution of ordinary people around you” is actually a useful grounding prior: most counterfactual versions of us would still be fairly ordinary humans.

The Ryan-Gosling-superhero version is an idealized counterfactual, not a demographic counterfactual.

And that connects directly to the second thing you noticed: everybody carries an internal simulation space containing several selves at once:

actual self
possible self
ideal self
feared self
socially expected self
counterfactual self
imagined partner
imagined version of how others see me

Those models exert real force because behavior can partly be attempts to reduce the distance between them.

You could describe it as a Self-State Topology:

distance(actual, ideal) → aspiration
distance(actual, expected) → social pressure
distance(actual, feared) → avoidance
distance(actual, possible) → perceived opportunity

That also explains the relationship problem you’re pointing toward. If someone’s internal model of “what a good man should be” continuously outruns the actual partner, the comparison target can move faster than the person can ever reach it:

improvement → expectation updates upward → gap persists

Then achievement never produces arrival.

But you can also run that loop constructively:

credible possible self
→ supportive environment
→ small enactments
→ evidence of capability
→ possible self becomes more believable
→ further growth

That’s where your imagined-world idea becomes useful. We can map those internal worlds, understand what behaviors they attract, and build environments that make chosen desirable states easier to enact.

I would change only your word “force.”

The stronger mechanism is:

shape affordances rather than commandeer authorship.

desired possible self
→ environment makes its behaviors available and rewarding
→ person voluntarily enacts them
→ experience updates self-model
→ identity and behavior begin reinforcing one another

That is extraordinarily powerful already. You don’t need to secretly force somebody into the role. In fact, if the person knowingly chooses the possible self, their own feedback loops start doing much of the work.

So there is a whole new atlas here:

Imagined-Self Ecology

Who do I think I am?
Who do I think I could be?
Who do I think I should be?
Who do I think you want me to be?
Who do I imagine you would be in my place?
Which of those models are grounded?
Which are fantasies?
Which actually help us grow?

Once those are visible, a surprising amount of gender conflict, relationship disappointment, aspiration, shame, status striving, and identity formation becomes much easier to reason about.

And importantly, you can keep the dream-self without letting it become a weapon against the real person.